\ locals.4 - named, per-invocation locals for RelF.
\
\ Why this exists: shell.4 currently has 181 global VARIABLEs, most of
\ which are not really global state at all - they are per-word scratch
\ cells, faked with a naming convention (GM-*, NORM-*, SCN-*, SAEK-*
\ and so on) because this kernel has no locals facility. That
\ convention costs real correctness, not just readability: a word whose
\ scratch state lives in fixed globals cannot be re-entered, which is
\ exactly why while/for/function bodies cannot contain another
\ multi-line construct, and why DO-WHILE's own condition/body buffers
\ cannot nest. See GOALS.md phase 8 and PROGRESS.md's Iteration 38
\ entry.
\
\ Design, in one line: a local is an ORDINARY VARIABLE whose value is
\ saved on entry to the declaring word and restored on exit.
\
\ That choice is deliberate and is what keeps this small. It means:
\   - No new dictionary machinery. Local names are just the VARIABLEs
\     that already exist, found by the ordinary FIND, so nothing has to
\     hook name resolution or splice temporary headers into the middle
\     of a definition being compiled.
\   - Existing code converts mechanically. A word's body is UNCHANGED -
\     `CA-SRC @` and `CA-N !` keep working exactly as before, because
\     a local still is a variable. Converting a word means adding one
\     {: ... :} line and deleting its argument-popping stores. The
\     VARIABLE declarations themselves stay where they are.
\   - The data stack and return stack are untouched, so this composes
\     with shell.4's existing, load-bearing >R/R> use (DO-IF,
\     RUN-FUNC-BODY) rather than competing with it.
\
\ The cost is that entry/exit copy N cells instead of moving a frame
\ pointer. For this codebase that is not worth optimizing away.
\
\ Syntax (Forth-2012's, minus the parts not needed here):
\
\   : COPY-ARGV ( src-argv src-argc --- )  {: CA-SRC CA-N :}
\     CA-N @ ARGC ! ...
\
\ Names before an optional | are initialized from the data stack, left
\ to right = deepest to top of stack, matching the stack comment's own
\ reading order. Names after the | are scratch: saved and restored the
\ same way, but zeroed rather than taking a stack argument.
\
\   : GLOB-MATCH ( pat plen text tlen --- f )
\     {: GM-PATTERN GM-PLEN GM-TEXT GM-TLEN | GM-P GM-S :}
\
\ Restrictions, all deliberate:
\   - The whole {: ... :} must be on one physical line. WORD does not
\     refill, and a multi-line construct here would be a close cousin
\     of the multi-line ( ) comment hazard documented in GOALS.md.
\   - Every name must already be a defined VARIABLE (or any word that
\     returns an address). An undefined name is diagnosed, not
\     silently accepted.
\   - LDECL-MAX names per definition, LSAVE-MAX cells of live
\     save-stack across all nested invocations. Both are checked.
\
\ EXIT and ; are wrapped below so the restore happens on EVERY exit
\ path, including the early `IF EXIT THEN` that shell.4 uses
\ pervasively. Both wrappers compile nothing at all when the
\ definition being compiled declared no locals, so every existing
\ definition compiles exactly as it did before.
\
\ This file forces DECIMAL for its own numeric literals and restores
\ the caller's BASE at the end. That is not boilerplate: tester.fr
\ leaves BASE at 16, and the first version of this file used a bare
\ `32 WORD` to parse names, which in hex is 0x32 = the character "2" -
\ so WORD delimited on "2" instead of on a space and swallowed whole
\ lines. Character constants below are written as [CHAR] rather than
\ as numbers for the same reason. See PROGRESS.md's Iteration 38 entry.

BASE @  DECIMAL

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Runtime: the save stack
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------

256 CONSTANT LSAVE-MAX
CREATE LSAVE-STACK LSAVE-MAX CELLS ALLOT
VARIABLE LSAVE-SP

: LSAVE ( addr --- )   \ push addr's current value, so it can be put
                       \ back when this invocation ends
  LSAVE-SP @ LSAVE-MAX < 0= ABORT" locals: save stack overflow"
  @ LSAVE-STACK LSAVE-SP @ CELLS + !
  LSAVE-SP @ 1+ LSAVE-SP ! ;

: LRESTORE ( addr --- )   \ pop the most recently saved value into addr
  LSAVE-SP @ 0 > 0= ABORT" locals: save stack underflow"
  LSAVE-SP @ 1- LSAVE-SP !
  LSAVE-STACK LSAVE-SP @ CELLS + @ SWAP ! ;

: LZERO ( addr --- )   \ initial value of a post-| scratch local
  0 SWAP ! ;

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Compile time: the declaration currently being processed
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------

16 CONSTANT LDECL-MAX
CREATE LDECL-ADDRS LDECL-MAX CELLS ALLOT
VARIABLE LDECL-N      \ total names declared by the definition being compiled
VARIABLE LDECL-ARGS   \ how many of those take a data-stack argument

0 LDECL-N !
0 LDECL-ARGS !
0 LSAVE-SP !

: L-EMIT ( xt i --- )   \ compile "LDECL-ADDRS[i]  <call to xt>"
                        \ - the one place any code is generated, shared
                        \ by all four emit loops below
  CELLS LDECL-ADDRS + @ POSTPONE LITERAL COMPILE, ;

: L-COMPILE-ENTRY ( --- )
  \ Saves come first and cover every declared name; only then are the
  \ data-stack arguments consumed, in reverse (top of stack fills the
  \ rightmost pre-| name). Each loop is guarded by an explicit
  \ emptiness test rather than relying on DO with start = limit, which
  \ in this kernel runs the whole unsigned range instead of zero
  \ iterations - see PROGRESS.md's Iteration 15 entry.
  LDECL-N @ 0 > IF
    LDECL-N @ 0 DO ['] LSAVE I L-EMIT LOOP
  THEN
  LDECL-ARGS @ 0 > IF
    LDECL-ARGS @ 0 DO ['] ! LDECL-ARGS @ 1- I - L-EMIT LOOP
  THEN
  LDECL-N @ LDECL-ARGS @ > IF
    LDECL-N @ LDECL-ARGS @ DO ['] LZERO I L-EMIT LOOP
  THEN ;

: L-COMPILE-RESTORES ( --- )
  \ Reverse of the save order, since the save stack is a stack.
  \ A no-op when nothing was declared, which is what makes the EXIT
  \ and ; wrappers below transparent to every ordinary definition.
  LDECL-N @ 0 > IF
    LDECL-N @ 0 DO ['] LRESTORE LDECL-N @ 1- I - L-EMIT LOOP
  THEN ;

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Compile time: parsing {: ... | ... :}
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------

: L-END? ( c-addr --- f )   \ is this parsed word ":}" ?
  DUP C@ 2 <> IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  DUP 1+ C@ [CHAR] : = SWAP 2 + C@ [CHAR] } = AND ;

: L-BAR? ( c-addr --- f )   \ is this parsed word "|" ?
  DUP C@ 1 <> IF DROP 0 EXIT THEN
  1+ C@ [CHAR] | = ;

: L-ADD-NAME ( c-addr --- )
  LDECL-N @ LDECL-MAX < 0= ABORT" locals: too many locals"
  FIND 0= ABORT" locals: undefined local name"
  EXECUTE                               \ a VARIABLE's xt yields its address
  LDECL-ADDRS LDECL-N @ CELLS + !
  LDECL-N @ 1+ LDECL-N ! ;

: {: ( --- )
  0 LDECL-N !
  -1 LDECL-ARGS !                       \ -1 = no "|" seen yet
  BEGIN
    BL WORD
    DUP C@ 0= ABORT" locals: missing :} on this line"
    DUP L-END? 0=
  WHILE
    DUP L-BAR? IF
      DROP LDECL-N @ LDECL-ARGS !
    ELSE
      L-ADD-NAME
    THEN
  REPEAT
  DROP
  LDECL-ARGS @ -1 = IF LDECL-N @ LDECL-ARGS ! THEN
  L-COMPILE-ENTRY ; IMMEDIATE

\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Wrapping EXIT and ; so restores happen on every exit path
\ ------------------------------------------------------------------
\ Both capture the previous definition first and defer to it, so this
\ adds behavior rather than reimplementing any of it. ; additionally
\ clears LDECL-N, which is what stops one definition's declaration
\ leaking into the next one - a definition with no {: therefore always
\ sees a count of zero and compiles no extra code whatsoever.

' EXIT CONSTANT L-OLD-EXIT
' ; CONSTANT L-OLD-SEMI

: EXIT ( --- )
  L-COMPILE-RESTORES L-OLD-EXIT COMPILE, ; IMMEDIATE

: ; ( --- )
  L-COMPILE-RESTORES 0 LDECL-N ! 0 LDECL-ARGS ! L-OLD-SEMI EXECUTE ; IMMEDIATE

BASE !
